insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-questions$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"The analyst's job: from questions to decisions","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Analysts sell decisions, not charts\n\nA shop owner does not want \"a dashboard\". She wants to know **which branch to restock, which product to stop, and whether last month's promo paid off**. The analyst's job is to turn data into a decision someone can act on.\n\n## The process\n\n1. **Ask**: what decision is being made, by whom, and by when?\n2. **Get the data**: POS exports, spreadsheets, bank statements, forms. Know where each number comes from.\n3. **Clean**: fix types, duplicates, blanks and mistakes (most of the work).\n4. **Analyse**: summarise, compare, look for trends and outliers.\n5. **Share**: a short story with one clear message, the numbers that prove it, and a recommendation.\n6. **Act and measure**: did the decision work?\n\n## Four kinds of analysis\n\n- **Descriptive**: what happened? *Sales were ₦4.2m in October.*\n- **Diagnostic**: why? *Garki fell because the generator broke for a week.*\n- **Predictive**: what will happen? *December will be about 30% above October.*\n- **Prescriptive**: what should we do? *Stock 40% more rice in Wuse before the holidays.*\n\n## Good questions are SMART\n\nSpecific, measurable, action-oriented, relevant and time-bound. Weak: *\"How are sales?\"* Strong: *\"Which 5 products lost the most revenue in Q3 compared with Q2, by branch, so we can decide what to reorder for Q4?\"*\n\n## Tools\n\nSpreadsheets (Excel, Google Sheets) take you far. Then SQL to pull data from databases, a BI tool (Power BI, Looker Studio, Tableau) for dashboards, and Python for heavier work. CDM Studio's **Data** page lets you practise on your own files right now. In this course you will build the core calculations in code, so the logic behind every tool is clear.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-cleaning$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Cleaning messy data","kind":"code","task":"Write toNumber, tidyName and dedupe.","hint":"toNumber: if (typeof text === 'number') return text; const s = String(text).trim().replace(/^[₦N]/, '').replace(/[,\\s]/g, ''); if (s === '') return null; const n = Number(s); return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null","body":"## Real data is messy\n\nA POS export or a sheet many people type into usually has:\n\n- Money as text: `\"₦1,200\"`, `\"1200 \"`, `\"N1,200.50\"`\n- Names typed differently: `\"Indomie\"`, `\" indomie\"`, `\"INDOMIE \"`\n- Duplicate rows from exporting twice\n- Blank or impossible values (a negative quantity, a date in 2062)\n\nIf you sum before cleaning, the totals are simply wrong.\n\n## Rules of thumb\n\n1. **Keep the raw file** untouched; clean a copy.\n2. **Make each column one type**: numbers as numbers, dates as dates.\n3. **Standardise text**: trim spaces, consistent capitals, one name per thing.\n4. **Remove exact duplicates**, but check: two identical sales might be real.\n5. **Write down every rule** you applied, so others can trust the result.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `toNumber(text)`: remove `₦`, `N` at the start, commas and spaces, then convert. Return `null` if it is not a valid number. Real numbers pass through unchanged.\n2. `tidyName(text)`: trim, collapse inner spaces, and make it Title Case (`\" INDOMIE  noodles\"` → `\"Indomie Noodles\"`).\n3. `dedupe(rows)`: remove rows that are exactly the same as an earlier row (compare with `JSON.stringify`), keeping the first.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-stats$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Descriptive statistics that matter","kind":"code","task":"Write median, percentile and outliers.","hint":"percentile: const s = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b); const pos = p * (s.length - 1); const lo = Math.floor(pos), hi = Math.ceil(pos); return s[lo] + (s[hi] - s[lo]) * (pos - lo). median is percentile(values, 0.5).","body":"## Average is not enough\n\nIf 9 customers spend ₦5,000 and one spends ₦500,000, the **mean** is ₦54,500, which describes nobody. Use the right summary:\n\n- **Mean**: sum ÷ count. Good when values are fairly even.\n- **Median**: the middle value when sorted (average of the two middle ones for an even count). Not pulled by extremes: ₦5,000 above.\n- **Percentiles**: the 90th percentile is the value 90% of data is below. Useful for delivery times: *\"90% of orders arrive within 2 days.\"*\n- **Spread**: range (max − min), standard deviation, and the **interquartile range (IQR)** = Q3 − Q1 (the middle 50%).\n\n## Outliers\n\nValues far from the rest may be **errors** (an extra zero typed) or **important** (a big new customer). A common rule: a value is an outlier if it is below **Q1 − 1.5 × IQR** or above **Q3 + 1.5 × IQR**. Always check outliers before deleting them.\n\n## Percentile method\n\nMany tools use linear interpolation: sort the values; position = p × (n − 1); if it is not a whole number, take the value between the two neighbours in proportion. For p = 0.5 this gives the median.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `median(values)`\n2. `percentile(values, p)` with the interpolation above (p between 0 and 1)\n3. `outliers(values)`: the values outside Q1 − 1.5×IQR and Q3 + 1.5×IQR, in their original order (Q1 = 25th percentile, Q3 = 75th).\n\nDo not change the input array (sort a copy).","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-stats$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-stats","title":"Descriptive statistics that matter","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Mean, median, percentiles and spotting outliers with the IQR rule.","body":"## Average is not enough\n\nIf 9 customers spend ₦5,000 and one spends ₦500,000, the **mean** is ₦54,500, which describes nobody. Use the right summary:\n\n- **Mean**: sum ÷ count. Good when values are fairly even.\n- **Median**: the middle value when sorted (average of the two middle ones for an even count). Not pulled by extremes: ₦5,000 above.\n- **Percentiles**: the 90th percentile is the value 90% of data is below. Useful for delivery times: *\"90% of orders arrive within 2 days.\"*\n- **Spread**: range (max − min), standard deviation, and the **interquartile range (IQR)** = Q3 − Q1 (the middle 50%).\n\n## Outliers\n\nValues far from the rest may be **errors** (an extra zero typed) or **important** (a big new customer). A common rule: a value is an outlier if it is below **Q1 − 1.5 × IQR** or above **Q3 + 1.5 × IQR**. Always check outliers before deleting them.\n\n## Percentile method\n\nMany tools use linear interpolation: sort the values; position = p × (n − 1); if it is not a whole number, take the value between the two neighbours in proportion. For p = 0.5 this gives the median.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `median(values)`\n2. `percentile(values, p)` with the interpolation above (p between 0 and 1)\n3. `outliers(values)`: the values outside Q1 − 1.5×IQR and Q3 + 1.5×IQR, in their original order (Q1 = 25th percentile, Q3 = 75th).\n\nDo not change the input array (sort a copy).","task":"Write median, percentile and outliers.","starter":"<script>\n  function median(values) {\n  }\n  function percentile(values, p) {\n  }\n  function outliers(values) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"median of an odd count","test":"return median([5, 1, 3]) === 3"},{"label":"median of an even count","test":"return median([4, 1, 3, 2]) === 2.5"},{"label":"percentile interpolates","test":"return Math.abs(percentile([10, 20, 30, 40], 0.25) - 17.5) < 1e-9 && Math.abs(percentile([10, 20, 30, 40], 0.9) - 37) < 1e-9"},{"label":"outliers found with the IQR rule","test":"return JSON.stringify(outliers([5000, 5200, 4800, 500000, 5100, 4900, 5050])) === '[500000]'"},{"label":"input is not changed","test":"const a = [3, 1, 2]; median(a); percentile(a, 0.5); return a.join() === '3,1,2'"}],"hint":"percentile: const s = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b); const pos = p * (s.length - 1); const lo = Math.floor(pos), hi = Math.ceil(pos); return s[lo] + (s[hi] - s[lo]) * (pos - lo). median is percentile(values, 0.5)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-pivot$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Pivot tables: grouping and summarising","kind":"code","task":"Write pivot and totals.","hint":"pivot: const out = {}; for (const r of rows) { const a = r[rowKey], b = r[colKey]; out[a] = out[a] || {}; out[a][b] = (out[a][b] || 0) + r[valueKey] }","body":"## The most useful spreadsheet tool\n\nA **pivot table** answers questions like *\"total sales by branch and by month\"* in seconds. It:\n\n1. Groups rows by one category (rows of the pivot), and optionally a second (columns)\n2. Applies a measure to each group (sum, count, average)\n3. Adds totals\n\n## Shares tell the story\n\nAbsolute numbers are hard to compare; **shares** are easier: *\"Wuse is 38% of all sales.\"* Share = group total ÷ grand total.\n\n## Your task\n\nSales rows look like `{ branch, month, amount }`.\n\n1. `pivot(rows, rowKey, colKey, valueKey)`: return an object where `result[row][col]` is the **sum** of `valueKey` for that pair. Missing pairs are simply absent.\n2. `totals(rows, key, valueKey)`: an array of `{ name, total, share }` for each value of `key`, sorted by total, highest first. `share` is a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-pivot$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-pivot","title":"Pivot tables: grouping and summarising","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Build the logic of a pivot table: totals by row and column categories, plus share of total.","body":"## The most useful spreadsheet tool\n\nA **pivot table** answers questions like *\"total sales by branch and by month\"* in seconds. It:\n\n1. Groups rows by one category (rows of the pivot), and optionally a second (columns)\n2. Applies a measure to each group (sum, count, average)\n3. Adds totals\n\n## Shares tell the story\n\nAbsolute numbers are hard to compare; **shares** are easier: *\"Wuse is 38% of all sales.\"* Share = group total ÷ grand total.\n\n## Your task\n\nSales rows look like `{ branch, month, amount }`.\n\n1. `pivot(rows, rowKey, colKey, valueKey)`: return an object where `result[row][col]` is the **sum** of `valueKey` for that pair. Missing pairs are simply absent.\n2. `totals(rows, key, valueKey)`: an array of `{ name, total, share }` for each value of `key`, sorted by total, highest first. `share` is a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place.","task":"Write pivot and totals.","starter":"<script>\n  const SALES = [\n    { branch: 'Wuse', month: 'Sep', amount: 400 },\n    { branch: 'Wuse', month: 'Oct', amount: 600 },\n    { branch: 'Garki', month: 'Sep', amount: 300 },\n    { branch: 'Garki', month: 'Sep', amount: 100 },\n    { branch: 'Kubwa', month: 'Oct', amount: 200 },\n  ]\n  function pivot(rows, rowKey, colKey, valueKey) {\n  }\n  function totals(rows, key, valueKey) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"pivot sums each pair","test":"const p = pivot(SALES, 'branch', 'month', 'amount'); return p.Wuse.Sep === 400 && p.Wuse.Oct === 600 && p.Garki.Sep === 400"},{"label":"missing pairs are absent","test":"const p = pivot(SALES, 'branch', 'month', 'amount'); return p.Garki.Oct === undefined && p.Kubwa.Sep === undefined"},{"label":"totals sorted highest first","test":"return totals(SALES, 'branch', 'amount').map(t => t.name).join() === 'Wuse,Garki,Kubwa'"},{"label":"shares are percentages to 1 decimal","test":"const t = totals(SALES, 'branch', 'amount'); return t[0].share === 62.5 && t[2].share === 12.5"}],"hint":"pivot: const out = {}; for (const r of rows) { const a = r[rowKey], b = r[colKey]; out[a] = out[a] || {}; out[a][b] = (out[a][b] || 0) + r[valueKey] }"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-trends$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Trends: growth, moving averages and seasonality","kind":"code","task":"Write growth, movingAverage and cagr.","hint":"growth: values.slice(1).map((v, i) => values[i] === 0 ? null : Math.round((v - values[i]) / values[i] * 1000) / 10)","body":"## Is it really growing?\n\nDaily or weekly numbers bounce around. To see the real direction:\n\n- **Period growth**: (this − last) ÷ last × 100. October ₦4.2m vs September ₦3.5m is **+20%**.\n- **Year-on-year (YoY)**: compare with the same period last year, to remove **seasonality** (December is always busy; school fees peak in September and January).\n- **Moving average**: the average of the last n periods, recomputed each period. A 7-day moving average removes the weekday/weekend pattern.\n- **CAGR** (compound annual growth rate): the steady yearly rate that turns a start value into an end value over n years: `(end ÷ start)^(1/n) − 1`.\n\n## Careful with percentages\n\n- Growth from a tiny base looks huge: ₦10k → ₦30k is +200% but only ₦20k.\n- A fall of 50% needs a rise of 100% to recover.\n- Inflation: in a high-inflation year, sales up 20% in naira may be flat or down in real terms. Compare with inflation or with units sold.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `growth(values)`: an array of period-on-period growth percentages rounded to 1 decimal, starting from the second value (use `null` when the previous value is 0).\n2. `movingAverage(values, n)`: for each position from `n - 1` onward, the average of the last n values (so the result has `values.length - n + 1` items).\n3. `cagr(start, end, years)`: as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-trends$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-trends","title":"Trends: growth, moving averages and seasonality","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Period-on-period growth, compound growth and smoothing noisy daily sales with a moving average.","body":"## Is it really growing?\n\nDaily or weekly numbers bounce around. To see the real direction:\n\n- **Period growth**: (this − last) ÷ last × 100. October ₦4.2m vs September ₦3.5m is **+20%**.\n- **Year-on-year (YoY)**: compare with the same period last year, to remove **seasonality** (December is always busy; school fees peak in September and January).\n- **Moving average**: the average of the last n periods, recomputed each period. A 7-day moving average removes the weekday/weekend pattern.\n- **CAGR** (compound annual growth rate): the steady yearly rate that turns a start value into an end value over n years: `(end ÷ start)^(1/n) − 1`.\n\n## Careful with percentages\n\n- Growth from a tiny base looks huge: ₦10k → ₦30k is +200% but only ₦20k.\n- A fall of 50% needs a rise of 100% to recover.\n- Inflation: in a high-inflation year, sales up 20% in naira may be flat or down in real terms. Compare with inflation or with units sold.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `growth(values)`: an array of period-on-period growth percentages rounded to 1 decimal, starting from the second value (use `null` when the previous value is 0).\n2. `movingAverage(values, n)`: for each position from `n - 1` onward, the average of the last n values (so the result has `values.length - n + 1` items).\n3. `cagr(start, end, years)`: as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal.","task":"Write growth, movingAverage and cagr.","starter":"<script>\n  function growth(values) {\n  }\n  function movingAverage(values, n) {\n  }\n  function cagr(start, end, years) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"growth period on period","test":"return JSON.stringify(growth([100, 120, 90, 90])) === '[20,-25,0]'"},{"label":"growth from zero is null","test":"return JSON.stringify(growth([0, 50])) === '[null]'"},{"label":"3-period moving average","test":"return JSON.stringify(movingAverage([3, 6, 9, 12, 3], 3)) === '[6,9,8]'"},{"label":"CAGR","test":"return cagr(1000000, 2000000, 3) === 26 && cagr(100, 100, 5) === 0"}],"hint":"growth: values.slice(1).map((v, i) => values[i] === 0 ? null : Math.round((v - values[i]) / values[i] * 1000) / 10)"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-kpis$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"KPIs and dashboards","kind":"code","task":"Write avgBasket, grossMargin, repeatRate and stockCover.","hint":"grossMargin: const r = sum of revenue, c = sum of cost; Math.round((r - c) / r * 1000) / 10. repeatRate: count orders per customer in an object.","body":"## Measure what drives the business\n\nA **KPI** (key performance indicator) is a number that tells you if the business is on track. Good KPIs are few, clear, owned by someone, and tied to a target. Examples for a shop:\n\n- **Revenue** and **gross margin %** = (revenue − cost of goods) ÷ revenue\n- **Average basket (order value)** = revenue ÷ number of orders\n- **Repeat customer rate** = customers with 2+ orders ÷ all customers\n- **Stock cover (days)** = stock on hand ÷ average daily units sold: *how many days until we run out?*\n- **Conversion rate** for online shops: orders ÷ visitors\n\n## Dashboard design\n\n- Put the 3 to 5 most important numbers at the top, each with its **comparison** (vs last month, vs target)\n- One chart per question; bars for comparing categories, lines for time\n- Same scales and colours everywhere; red only for real problems\n- Update automatically if you can; a dashboard nobody trusts is worse than none\n\n## Your task\n\nOrders look like `{ customer, revenue, cost }`.\n\n1. `avgBasket(orders)`: revenue ÷ orders, rounded to whole naira.\n2. `grossMargin(orders)`: percentage rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `repeatRate(orders)`: percentage of distinct customers with 2 or more orders, rounded to 1 decimal.\n4. `stockCover(stock, unitsSoldLast30Days)`: days of cover rounded down; `Infinity` if nothing sold.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-kpis$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-kpis","title":"KPIs and dashboards","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Choose the right KPIs and compute average basket, repeat rate, gross margin and stock cover.","body":"## Measure what drives the business\n\nA **KPI** (key performance indicator) is a number that tells you if the business is on track. Good KPIs are few, clear, owned by someone, and tied to a target. Examples for a shop:\n\n- **Revenue** and **gross margin %** = (revenue − cost of goods) ÷ revenue\n- **Average basket (order value)** = revenue ÷ number of orders\n- **Repeat customer rate** = customers with 2+ orders ÷ all customers\n- **Stock cover (days)** = stock on hand ÷ average daily units sold: *how many days until we run out?*\n- **Conversion rate** for online shops: orders ÷ visitors\n\n## Dashboard design\n\n- Put the 3 to 5 most important numbers at the top, each with its **comparison** (vs last month, vs target)\n- One chart per question; bars for comparing categories, lines for time\n- Same scales and colours everywhere; red only for real problems\n- Update automatically if you can; a dashboard nobody trusts is worse than none\n\n## Your task\n\nOrders look like `{ customer, revenue, cost }`.\n\n1. `avgBasket(orders)`: revenue ÷ orders, rounded to whole naira.\n2. `grossMargin(orders)`: percentage rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `repeatRate(orders)`: percentage of distinct customers with 2 or more orders, rounded to 1 decimal.\n4. `stockCover(stock, unitsSoldLast30Days)`: days of cover rounded down; `Infinity` if nothing sold.","task":"Write avgBasket, grossMargin, repeatRate and stockCover.","starter":"<script>\n  const ORDERS = [\n    { customer: 'Ada', revenue: 5000, cost: 3500 },\n    { customer: 'Bayo', revenue: 12000, cost: 9000 },\n    { customer: 'Ada', revenue: 3000, cost: 2000 },\n    { customer: 'Chi', revenue: 8000, cost: 5500 },\n  ]\n  function avgBasket(orders) {\n  }\n  function grossMargin(orders) {\n  }\n  function repeatRate(orders) {\n  }\n  function stockCover(stock, unitsSoldLast30Days) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"average basket","test":"return avgBasket(ORDERS) === 7000"},{"label":"gross margin %","test":"return grossMargin(ORDERS) === 28.6"},{"label":"repeat customer rate","test":"return repeatRate(ORDERS) === 33.3"},{"label":"stock cover in days","test":"return stockCover(100, 60) === 50 && stockCover(10, 0) === Infinity"}],"hint":"grossMargin: const r = sum of revenue, c = sum of cost; Math.round((r - c) / r * 1000) / 10. repeatRate: count orders per customer in an object."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-story$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Data storytelling and your analyst career","kind":"prompt","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## The story structure that works\n\nBusy managers read the first two lines. Lead with the answer:\n\n1. **Headline**: the one message. *\"Garki is losing ₦600k a month in stock-outs of 5 fast movers.\"*\n2. **Evidence**: 2 or 3 numbers or one clear chart that prove it.\n3. **Why it matters**: money, customers, risk.\n4. **Recommendation**: what to do, who, by when, and what it will cost or save.\n5. **Next check**: how you will know it worked.\n\nAvoid: 20 charts, jargon, numbers without comparison (\"₦4m\": good or bad?), and hiding the uncertainty. Say what you are not sure about.\n\n## Charts that tell the truth\n\n- Bar charts start at zero\n- Do not use 3D or pie charts with many slices\n- Label directly; title the chart with the insight, not the field name (\"Sales fell 18% after the price rise\", not \"Sales by month\")\n\n## Getting into analytics\n\n- Master **Excel / Google Sheets** (pivot tables, XLOOKUP, charts), then **SQL**, then **Power BI** or **Looker Studio**, then Python\n- Build 3 portfolio projects on real-looking data: a sales dashboard, a customer analysis, a forecast. Publish them with a one-page write-up each.\n- Certificates employers know: **Google Data Analytics**, **Microsoft PL-300 (Power BI)**; your CDM Studio certificate shows the foundations\n- Freelance on Upwork or Fiverr: small businesses need monthly sales reports and dashboards\n\n## Practise\n\nAsk the tutor to help you turn a set of numbers into a one-page story with a recommendation.","practice":"Give the AI some numbers from a business you know (for example, monthly sales for 3 branches for 4 months, with one branch falling), and ask it to help you write a one-page story: headline, the evidence, why it matters, a recommendation with owner and deadline, and how to check it worked. Then ask it to critique your headline.","track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-story$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-story","title":"Data storytelling and your analyst career","minutes":20,"kind":"prompt","outline":"Turn analysis into a one-page story with a recommendation, and plan your path into analytics jobs.","body":"## The story structure that works\n\nBusy managers read the first two lines. Lead with the answer:\n\n1. **Headline**: the one message. *\"Garki is losing ₦600k a month in stock-outs of 5 fast movers.\"*\n2. **Evidence**: 2 or 3 numbers or one clear chart that prove it.\n3. **Why it matters**: money, customers, risk.\n4. **Recommendation**: what to do, who, by when, and what it will cost or save.\n5. **Next check**: how you will know it worked.\n\nAvoid: 20 charts, jargon, numbers without comparison (\"₦4m\": good or bad?), and hiding the uncertainty. Say what you are not sure about.\n\n## Charts that tell the truth\n\n- Bar charts start at zero\n- Do not use 3D or pie charts with many slices\n- Label directly; title the chart with the insight, not the field name (\"Sales fell 18% after the price rise\", not \"Sales by month\")\n\n## Getting into analytics\n\n- Master **Excel / Google Sheets** (pivot tables, XLOOKUP, charts), then **SQL**, then **Power BI** or **Looker Studio**, then Python\n- Build 3 portfolio projects on real-looking data: a sales dashboard, a customer analysis, a forecast. Publish them with a one-page write-up each.\n- Certificates employers know: **Google Data Analytics**, **Microsoft PL-300 (Power BI)**; your CDM Studio certificate shows the foundations\n- Freelance on Upwork or Fiverr: small businesses need monthly sales reports and dashboards\n\n## Practise\n\nAsk the tutor to help you turn a set of numbers into a one-page story with a recommendation.","practice":"Give the AI some numbers from a business you know (for example, monthly sales for 3 branches for 4 months, with one branch falling), and ask it to help you write a one-page story: headline, the evidence, why it matters, a recommendation with owner and deadline, and how to check it worked. Then ask it to critique your headline."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
