insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-mindset$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Think like an attacker, act like a defender","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Why security matters to everyone\n\nEvery business now runs on data: customer phone numbers, sales records, bank details, staff salaries. When that data is stolen, changed or locked away, the business can stop overnight. Cybersecurity is the work of keeping it **safe, correct and available**.\n\n## The CIA triad\n\nSecurity people check every system against three goals:\n\n- **Confidentiality**: only the right people can see the data. *A payroll file is readable by HR, not by every staff member.*\n- **Integrity**: the data is correct and nobody changed it without permission. *A cashier cannot quietly lower a price after a sale.*\n- **Availability**: the system works when it is needed. *The shop's POS is up on a busy Saturday.*\n\nWhen you hear about an attack, ask which of the three it hit. Ransomware hits availability (and often confidentiality). A fake bank alert hits integrity. A leaked customer list hits confidentiality.\n\n## Threat, vulnerability, risk\n\n- A **threat** is anything that could cause harm: a scammer, a careless employee, a flood, a power cut.\n- A **vulnerability** is a weakness the threat can use: a reused password, an unpatched phone, a door with no lock.\n- **Risk** = how likely the harm is × how bad it would be.\n\n> You cannot remove every risk. Security is choosing which risks to reduce first, with the time and money you have.\n\n## Thinking like an attacker\n\nAttackers look for the **easiest way in**, not the cleverest. Most real attacks in small businesses start with:\n\n1. A **stolen or guessed password** (often reused from another site that was hacked)\n2. A **phishing message** that tricks someone into clicking, paying or sharing a code\n3. **Unpatched software** with a known hole\n4. **Too much access**: everyone uses the admin account\n\nA defender's job is to make each of these harder, and to notice quickly when something goes wrong.\n\n## Defence in depth\n\nNever trust a single lock. Layer your protections so that if one fails, the next one still stops the attacker:\n\n- Strong, unique passwords **and** two-step sign-in\n- Staff who can spot scams **and** limits on what each account can do\n- Backups **and** a tested plan for restoring them\n\n## The least privilege rule\n\nGive every person and every app **only the access they need, for only as long as they need it**. A cashier needs to sell; she does not need to delete the sales history. This one rule stops a huge share of damage.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-passwords$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Passwords, password managers and two-step sign-in","kind":"code","task":"Finish strength(p) so it returns a score from 0 to 4 using the rules above.","hint":"let s = 0; if (p.length >= 12) s++; if (/[a-z]/.test(p) && /[A-Z]/.test(p)) s++; if (/[0-9]/.test(p)) s++; if (/[^A-Za-z0-9]/.test(p)) s++. Check COMMON first with p.toLowerCase().includes(word).","body":"## Why passwords fail\n\nAttackers rarely \"guess\" a password one by one. They use:\n\n- **Credential stuffing**: trying email + password pairs leaked from other sites. If you reuse passwords, one leak opens all your accounts.\n- **Dictionary attacks**: millions of common passwords and words per second (`password123`, `Lagos2024`, your child's name).\n- **Phishing**: simply asking you for it on a fake page.\n\n## What actually makes a password strong\n\n1. **Length beats complexity.** `correct-horse-battery-staple` is far stronger than `P@ss1!`.\n2. **Unique for every site.** Then a leak somewhere else does not matter.\n3. **Not personal.** No names, birthdays, phone numbers or your business name.\n\nThe easy way to do all three: a **password manager** (Bitwarden, 1Password, the one built into your phone). It makes and remembers long random passwords; you remember one strong passphrase.\n\n## Two-step sign-in (2FA)\n\nEven a perfect password can be phished. Two-step sign-in adds something you **have** (an authenticator app code) to something you **know**. Prefer an authenticator app over SMS codes, which can be stolen with a SIM swap. **Never** read a code to anyone who calls you, even \"your bank\".\n\n## Your task: a strength checker\n\nMany sign-up forms show how strong a password is. You will write the logic. Score a password from 0 to 4:\n\n- +1 if it is at least **12** characters long\n- +1 if it has **both** lower case and upper case letters\n- +1 if it has a **digit**\n- +1 if it has a **symbol** (anything that is not a letter or digit)\n- But if it contains a common word from the `COMMON` list (ignore case), the score is **0** no matter what.\n\nUse regular expressions: `/[a-z]/.test(p)` is true when `p` has a lower case letter.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-phishing$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Phishing, scams and social engineering","kind":"code","task":"Write isTrusted(url, trusted) using new URL(...).protocol and .hostname.","hint":"try { const u = new URL(url); if (u.protocol !== 'https:') return false; return trusted.some(d => u.hostname === d || u.hostname.endsWith('.' + d)) } catch { return false }","body":"## The human is the target\n\n**Social engineering** means tricking people instead of breaking computers. It works because it uses normal human feelings: fear, urgency, greed, helpfulness and respect for authority.\n\nCommon forms:\n\n- **Phishing** emails and **smishing** (SMS) that copy a bank, a delivery company or your boss\n- **Vishing**: phone calls from \"customer care\" asking for your OTP or PIN\n- **Fake payment alerts**: a doctored screenshot or SMS saying money was sent\n- **Business email compromise**: a message that looks like it is from the MD: *\"Pay this supplier urgently, I'm in a meeting.\"*\n\n## Red flags\n\n1. **Urgency or threat**: \"Your account will be blocked in 1 hour.\"\n2. **A request for secrets**: OTP, PIN, password, BVN, card details. Real banks never ask.\n3. **A link that does not match**: the text says one thing, the address goes somewhere else.\n4. **A look-alike domain**: `paystack-verify.com`, `micros0ft.com`, `gtbank.secure-login.ng`.\n5. **A change of bank details** for a supplier, by email only.\n\n## Reading a web address\n\nIn `https://login.bank.com.evil.ng/reset`, the real site is decided by the **end of the host name**: here it is `evil.ng`, not `bank.com`. Everything before it is just a label the attacker chose.\n\n## The rule that saves money\n\n**Verify through a second channel.** If a message asks for money or secrets, call the person or company on a number you already have (not the one in the message).\n\n## Your task: a link checker\n\nWrite `isTrusted(url, trusted)` that returns true only when the link is **https** and its host **is** a trusted domain or **ends with \".\" + a trusted domain** (so `www.paystack.com` is fine, `paystack.com.evil.ng` is not). Use `new URL(url)`, which throws on nonsense, so wrap it in try/catch and return false.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-phishing$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-phishing","title":"Phishing, scams and social engineering","minutes":20,"kind":"code","outline":"How scams work, the red flags, and build a link checker that spots look-alike domains.","body":"## The human is the target\n\n**Social engineering** means tricking people instead of breaking computers. It works because it uses normal human feelings: fear, urgency, greed, helpfulness and respect for authority.\n\nCommon forms:\n\n- **Phishing** emails and **smishing** (SMS) that copy a bank, a delivery company or your boss\n- **Vishing**: phone calls from \"customer care\" asking for your OTP or PIN\n- **Fake payment alerts**: a doctored screenshot or SMS saying money was sent\n- **Business email compromise**: a message that looks like it is from the MD: *\"Pay this supplier urgently, I'm in a meeting.\"*\n\n## Red flags\n\n1. **Urgency or threat**: \"Your account will be blocked in 1 hour.\"\n2. **A request for secrets**: OTP, PIN, password, BVN, card details. Real banks never ask.\n3. **A link that does not match**: the text says one thing, the address goes somewhere else.\n4. **A look-alike domain**: `paystack-verify.com`, `micros0ft.com`, `gtbank.secure-login.ng`.\n5. **A change of bank details** for a supplier, by email only.\n\n## Reading a web address\n\nIn `https://login.bank.com.evil.ng/reset`, the real site is decided by the **end of the host name**: here it is `evil.ng`, not `bank.com`. Everything before it is just a label the attacker chose.\n\n## The rule that saves money\n\n**Verify through a second channel.** If a message asks for money or secrets, call the person or company on a number you already have (not the one in the message).\n\n## Your task: a link checker\n\nWrite `isTrusted(url, trusted)` that returns true only when the link is **https** and its host **is** a trusted domain or **ends with \".\" + a trusted domain** (so `www.paystack.com` is fine, `paystack.com.evil.ng` is not). Use `new URL(url)`, which throws on nonsense, so wrap it in try/catch and return false.","task":"Write isTrusted(url, trusted) using new URL(...).protocol and .hostname.","starter":"<h2>Is this link safe?</h2>\n<script>\n  const TRUSTED = ['paystack.com', 'gtbank.com', 'cdmtechnologies.github.io']\n\n  function isTrusted(url, trusted) {\n    // your code\n    return false\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"the real site is trusted","test":"return isTrusted('https://paystack.com/pay', TRUSTED) === true"},{"label":"a sub-domain of a trusted site is fine","test":"return isTrusted('https://www.gtbank.com/login', TRUSTED) === true"},{"label":"a look-alike ending in another domain is refused","test":"return isTrusted('https://paystack.com.verify-now.ng/pay', TRUSTED) === false"},{"label":"a name that only contains the domain is refused","test":"return isTrusted('https://mypaystack.com', TRUSTED) === false"},{"label":"http (not secure) is refused","test":"return isTrusted('http://paystack.com', TRUSTED) === false"},{"label":"nonsense is refused without crashing","test":"return isTrusted('not a link', TRUSTED) === false"}],"hint":"try { const u = new URL(url); if (u.protocol !== 'https:') return false; return trusted.some(d => u.hostname === d || u.hostname.endsWith('.' + d)) } catch { return false }"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-crypto$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Hashing and encryption in practice","kind":"code","task":"Write sha256(text) returning lower-case hex, and sameFile(a, b) using it.","hint":"const d = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(text)); return [...new Uint8Array(d)].map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('')","body":"## Two different tools\n\n- **Encryption** scrambles data with a **key** so it can be **unscrambled** later by someone with the key. Use it for data you need to read again: messages, backups, files.\n- **Hashing** turns data into a fixed-length \"fingerprint\" that **cannot** be turned back. The same input always gives the same hash; a tiny change gives a completely different hash.\n\n## Where hashing is used\n\n- **Storing passwords**: a site should never store your password, only a slow, salted hash of it. When you sign in, it hashes what you typed and compares.\n- **Checking files were not changed**: download pages publish the SHA-256 of a file; you hash your copy and compare.\n- **Fingerprints** in apps: Android apps are identified by the SHA-256 of their signing certificate.\n\n## Salts and slow hashes\n\nIf two people use the same password, they would get the same hash, and attackers keep huge tables of hashes for common passwords. A **salt** (random text added to each password before hashing) makes every hash unique. Password hashing must also be **slow** (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2) so guessing billions is impractical. Plain SHA-256 is fast: great for fingerprints, wrong for passwords on its own.\n\n## Encryption you use every day\n\n- **HTTPS** encrypts everything between the browser and the site. Never type a password on a page without it.\n- **End-to-end encryption** (WhatsApp, Signal) means only the two phones can read messages.\n- **Disk encryption** (BitLocker, FileVault, phone encryption) protects a stolen laptop.\n\n## Your task: SHA-256 in the browser\n\nBrowsers include the Web Crypto API. Hashing is **asynchronous** (it returns a Promise):\n\n```js\nconst bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello')\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes)   // an ArrayBuffer\n```\n\nWrite `async function sha256(text)` that returns the hash as **64 lower-case hex characters**. Turn each byte into 2 hex digits: `b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')`.\n\nThen write `async function sameFile(a, b)` that returns true when the two texts have the same hash.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-crypto$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-crypto","title":"Hashing and encryption in practice","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Hashing vs encryption, salts, HTTPS, and hash data with the browser's Web Crypto API.","body":"## Two different tools\n\n- **Encryption** scrambles data with a **key** so it can be **unscrambled** later by someone with the key. Use it for data you need to read again: messages, backups, files.\n- **Hashing** turns data into a fixed-length \"fingerprint\" that **cannot** be turned back. The same input always gives the same hash; a tiny change gives a completely different hash.\n\n## Where hashing is used\n\n- **Storing passwords**: a site should never store your password, only a slow, salted hash of it. When you sign in, it hashes what you typed and compares.\n- **Checking files were not changed**: download pages publish the SHA-256 of a file; you hash your copy and compare.\n- **Fingerprints** in apps: Android apps are identified by the SHA-256 of their signing certificate.\n\n## Salts and slow hashes\n\nIf two people use the same password, they would get the same hash, and attackers keep huge tables of hashes for common passwords. A **salt** (random text added to each password before hashing) makes every hash unique. Password hashing must also be **slow** (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2) so guessing billions is impractical. Plain SHA-256 is fast: great for fingerprints, wrong for passwords on its own.\n\n## Encryption you use every day\n\n- **HTTPS** encrypts everything between the browser and the site. Never type a password on a page without it.\n- **End-to-end encryption** (WhatsApp, Signal) means only the two phones can read messages.\n- **Disk encryption** (BitLocker, FileVault, phone encryption) protects a stolen laptop.\n\n## Your task: SHA-256 in the browser\n\nBrowsers include the Web Crypto API. Hashing is **asynchronous** (it returns a Promise):\n\n```js\nconst bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello')\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes)   // an ArrayBuffer\n```\n\nWrite `async function sha256(text)` that returns the hash as **64 lower-case hex characters**. Turn each byte into 2 hex digits: `b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')`.\n\nThen write `async function sameFile(a, b)` that returns true when the two texts have the same hash.","task":"Write sha256(text) returning lower-case hex, and sameFile(a, b) using it.","starter":"<h2>Fingerprints</h2>\n<script>\n  async function sha256(text) {\n    // your code\n  }\n\n  async function sameFile(a, b) {\n    // your code\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"sha256 of \"hello\" is right","test":"return (await sha256('hello')) === '2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824'"},{"label":"it is 64 hex characters","test":"return /^[0-9a-f]{64}$/.test(await sha256('CDM Studio'))"},{"label":"one letter changes everything","test":"return (await sha256('Hello')) !== (await sha256('hello'))"},{"label":"sameFile spots identical text","test":"return (await sameFile('invoice 42', 'invoice 42')) === true"},{"label":"sameFile spots a change","test":"return (await sameFile('pay 5000', 'pay 50000')) === false"}],"hint":"const d = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(text)); return [...new Uint8Array(d)].map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('')"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-xss$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Secure web code: stop XSS","kind":"code","task":"Write escapeHtml, make addComment safe with textContent, and write validName.","hint":"escapeHtml: s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;').replace(/\"/g, '&quot;').replace(/'/g, '&#39;'). addComment: const li = document.createElement('li'); li.textContent = text; wall.appendChild(li)","body":"## Untrusted input is dangerous\n\nAny text that comes from a user (a name, a comment, a search, a product review) may contain code. If your page puts that text into the HTML as **HTML**, the browser runs it. This is **cross-site scripting (XSS)**, one of the most common web attacks.\n\nExample: a comment wall does\n\n```js\nwall.innerHTML += '<li>' + comment + '</li>'\n```\n\nand someone posts `<img src=x onerror=\"steal()\">`. Every visitor's browser now runs `steal()`, which can read what they type, change prices on the page or send their session to the attacker.\n\n## The fixes\n\n1. **Treat input as text, not HTML.** Use `element.textContent = value`: the browser shows it as letters, never runs it.\n2. **When you must build HTML strings, escape** the five special characters: `&` → `&amp;`, `<` → `&lt;`, `>` → `&gt;`, `\"` → `&quot;`, `'` → `&#39;`. Escape `&` **first**, or you will escape your own escapes.\n3. **Check input on the server too.** Anything in the browser can be bypassed; the server must check lengths, formats and permissions again.\n4. **Never put user input into** `eval`, `new Function`, `setTimeout(\"string\")` or `href=\"javascript:...\"`.\n\n## Validation vs escaping\n\n- **Validation** says *\"is this allowed?\"* (an 11-digit phone, an email, a number between 1 and 100). Reject what does not fit.\n- **Escaping** says *\"show it safely\"*. You need both.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. Write `escapeHtml(s)` that escapes all five characters.\n2. Fix `addComment(text)` so it adds an `<li>` whose **textContent** is the text (no innerHTML with the comment).\n3. Write `validName(s)`: true only for 2 to 40 characters made of letters, spaces, hyphens and apostrophes (`/^[A-Za-z' -]{2,40}$/`).","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-xss$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-xss","title":"Secure web code: stop XSS","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"How cross-site scripting works, and make a comment wall safe by escaping and using textContent.","body":"## Untrusted input is dangerous\n\nAny text that comes from a user (a name, a comment, a search, a product review) may contain code. If your page puts that text into the HTML as **HTML**, the browser runs it. This is **cross-site scripting (XSS)**, one of the most common web attacks.\n\nExample: a comment wall does\n\n```js\nwall.innerHTML += '<li>' + comment + '</li>'\n```\n\nand someone posts `<img src=x onerror=\"steal()\">`. Every visitor's browser now runs `steal()`, which can read what they type, change prices on the page or send their session to the attacker.\n\n## The fixes\n\n1. **Treat input as text, not HTML.** Use `element.textContent = value`: the browser shows it as letters, never runs it.\n2. **When you must build HTML strings, escape** the five special characters: `&` → `&amp;`, `<` → `&lt;`, `>` → `&gt;`, `\"` → `&quot;`, `'` → `&#39;`. Escape `&` **first**, or you will escape your own escapes.\n3. **Check input on the server too.** Anything in the browser can be bypassed; the server must check lengths, formats and permissions again.\n4. **Never put user input into** `eval`, `new Function`, `setTimeout(\"string\")` or `href=\"javascript:...\"`.\n\n## Validation vs escaping\n\n- **Validation** says *\"is this allowed?\"* (an 11-digit phone, an email, a number between 1 and 100). Reject what does not fit.\n- **Escaping** says *\"show it safely\"*. You need both.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. Write `escapeHtml(s)` that escapes all five characters.\n2. Fix `addComment(text)` so it adds an `<li>` whose **textContent** is the text (no innerHTML with the comment).\n3. Write `validName(s)`: true only for 2 to 40 characters made of letters, spaces, hyphens and apostrophes (`/^[A-Za-z' -]{2,40}$/`).","task":"Write escapeHtml, make addComment safe with textContent, and write validName.","starter":"<h2>Comment wall</h2>\n<ul id=\"wall\"></ul>\n<script>\n  const wall = document.getElementById('wall')\n\n  function escapeHtml(s) {\n    // your code\n  }\n\n  function addComment(text) {\n    // UNSAFE: fix this line\n    wall.innerHTML += '<li>' + text + '</li>'\n  }\n\n  function validName(s) {\n    // your code\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"escapeHtml escapes < and >","test":"return escapeHtml('<b>') === '&lt;b&gt;'"},{"label":"escapeHtml escapes & first, then quotes","test":"return escapeHtml(`a & \"b\" 'c'`) === 'a &amp; &quot;b&quot; &#39;c&#39;'"},{"label":"a comment with code is shown as text","test":"wall.innerHTML = ''; addComment('<img src=x id=evil>'); return !document.getElementById('evil') && wall.textContent.includes('<img')"},{"label":"each comment is its own <li>","test":"wall.innerHTML = ''; addComment('one'); addComment('two'); return wall.querySelectorAll('li').length === 2"},{"label":"validName accepts real names","test":"return validName(\"Ngozi O'Neil-Ade\") === true"},{"label":"validName refuses code and too short","test":"return validName('<script>') === false && validName('A') === false"}],"hint":"escapeHtml: s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;').replace(/\"/g, '&quot;').replace(/'/g, '&#39;'). addComment: const li = document.createElement('li'); li.textContent = text; wall.appendChild(li)"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-access$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Access control and rate limiting","kind":"code","task":"Write can(role, action) and attempt(user, ok, now) as described.","hint":"can: return (ROLES[role] || []).includes(action). attempt: const recent = (fails[user] || []).filter(x => now - x < WINDOW); if (recent.length >= 5) return 'blocked'; if (ok) { fails[user] = []; return 'ok' } fails[user] = [...recent, now]; return 'fail'","body":"## Authentication vs authorisation\n\n- **Authentication**: *who are you?* (password, two-step code, fingerprint)\n- **Authorisation**: *what may you do?* (sell, refund, see salaries, change prices)\n\nMost serious breaches inside apps are authorisation mistakes: a cashier can open the admin page by typing its address, or changing an ID in a link shows someone else's invoice (called **IDOR**, insecure direct object reference). The fix is to **check permissions on the server for every request**, not just hide buttons.\n\n## Roles\n\nGroup permissions into roles, and give people roles:\n\n| Role | Can |\n|---|---|\n| cashier | sell |\n| manager | sell, refund, see reports |\n| owner | everything |\n\nDefault to **deny**: if a role is unknown, or a permission is not listed, the answer is no.\n\n## Brute force and rate limiting\n\nAn attacker can try thousands of passwords or OTP codes per minute. **Rate limiting** caps attempts: for example, after 5 failed sign-ins in 15 minutes, block that account (or IP) for a while. Always show the **same** message for \"wrong email\" and \"wrong password\" so attackers cannot learn which emails exist.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. Write `can(role, action)` using the `ROLES` table. Unknown roles or actions return **false**.\n2. Write a limiter: `attempt(user, ok, now)` records a sign-in attempt at time `now` (milliseconds). It returns **'blocked'** if the user already has **5 failed attempts within the last 15 minutes** (before this one), otherwise **'ok'** when `ok` is true or **'fail'** when false. A successful sign-in clears that user's failures.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-access$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-access","title":"Access control and rate limiting","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Roles and permissions, brute-force protection, and build a role check plus a login limiter.","body":"## Authentication vs authorisation\n\n- **Authentication**: *who are you?* (password, two-step code, fingerprint)\n- **Authorisation**: *what may you do?* (sell, refund, see salaries, change prices)\n\nMost serious breaches inside apps are authorisation mistakes: a cashier can open the admin page by typing its address, or changing an ID in a link shows someone else's invoice (called **IDOR**, insecure direct object reference). The fix is to **check permissions on the server for every request**, not just hide buttons.\n\n## Roles\n\nGroup permissions into roles, and give people roles:\n\n| Role | Can |\n|---|---|\n| cashier | sell |\n| manager | sell, refund, see reports |\n| owner | everything |\n\nDefault to **deny**: if a role is unknown, or a permission is not listed, the answer is no.\n\n## Brute force and rate limiting\n\nAn attacker can try thousands of passwords or OTP codes per minute. **Rate limiting** caps attempts: for example, after 5 failed sign-ins in 15 minutes, block that account (or IP) for a while. Always show the **same** message for \"wrong email\" and \"wrong password\" so attackers cannot learn which emails exist.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. Write `can(role, action)` using the `ROLES` table. Unknown roles or actions return **false**.\n2. Write a limiter: `attempt(user, ok, now)` records a sign-in attempt at time `now` (milliseconds). It returns **'blocked'** if the user already has **5 failed attempts within the last 15 minutes** (before this one), otherwise **'ok'** when `ok` is true or **'fail'** when false. A successful sign-in clears that user's failures.","task":"Write can(role, action) and attempt(user, ok, now) as described.","starter":"<script>\n  const ROLES = {\n    cashier: ['sell'],\n    manager: ['sell', 'refund', 'reports'],\n    owner: ['sell', 'refund', 'reports', 'prices', 'staff'],\n  }\n\n  function can(role, action) {\n    // your code\n  }\n\n  const WINDOW = 15 * 60 * 1000\n  const fails = {} // user -> array of times of failed attempts\n\n  function attempt(user, ok, now) {\n    // your code\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"a cashier can sell","test":"return can('cashier', 'sell') === true"},{"label":"a cashier cannot refund","test":"return can('cashier', 'refund') === false"},{"label":"unknown roles are denied","test":"return can('hacker', 'sell') === false && can('owner', 'delete_everything') === false"},{"label":"5 failures then blocked","test":"for (const k in fails) delete fails[k]; const t = 1e9; for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) attempt('ada', false, t + i); return attempt('ada', true, t + 10) === 'blocked'"},{"label":"old failures expire after 15 minutes","test":"for (const k in fails) delete fails[k]; const t = 2e9; for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) attempt('bayo', false, t); return attempt('bayo', true, t + 16 * 60 * 1000) === 'ok'"},{"label":"success clears failures","test":"for (const k in fails) delete fails[k]; const t = 3e9; for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) attempt('chi', false, t); attempt('chi', true, t + 1); for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) attempt('chi', false, t + 2); return attempt('chi', false, t + 3) === 'fail'"}],"hint":"can: return (ROLES[role] || []).includes(action). attempt: const recent = (fails[user] || []).filter(x => now - x < WINDOW); if (recent.length >= 5) return 'blocked'; if (ok) { fails[user] = []; return 'ok' } fails[user] = [...recent, now]; return 'fail'"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-network$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Networks, Wi-Fi and devices","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## How data travels\n\nYour phone talks to a website through many networks: your Wi-Fi router, your internet provider, undersea cables, the website's host. Anyone in the middle could read unencrypted traffic, which is why **HTTPS everywhere** matters.\n\nKey words:\n\n- **IP address**: the number that identifies a device on a network.\n- **Port**: a numbered \"door\" on a device for a service (443 for HTTPS, 22 for SSH).\n- **Firewall**: rules that decide which traffic may come in or go out. Close every port you do not need.\n- **VPN**: an encrypted tunnel, useful on public Wi-Fi and to reach office systems safely.\n\n## Wi-Fi for a shop or office\n\n1. Change the router's **default admin password** (attackers know them all).\n2. Use **WPA2 or WPA3** with a long passphrase. Never \"open\" or WEP.\n3. Put customers on a **guest network**, separate from the POS and office computers.\n4. Turn off **WPS** (the push-button pairing), which is easy to attack.\n5. Update the router's firmware.\n\n## Updates are security fixes\n\nMost attacks use holes that were **already fixed**. Turn on automatic updates for phones, computers, browsers and apps. Replace devices that no longer get updates.\n\n## Backups: the 3-2-1 rule\n\n- **3** copies of important data\n- on **2** different kinds of storage\n- with **1** copy **off-site** or offline (so ransomware cannot reach it)\n\nA backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. Test a restore every few months.\n\n## Phones and laptops\n\n- Screen lock with a PIN, fingerprint or face\n- Disk encryption on (most phones do this automatically with a screen lock)\n- Install apps only from the official store\n- \"Find my device\" on, so a lost phone can be locked or wiped\n- Separate staff accounts; no shared admin passwords\n\n## Public Wi-Fi\n\nAssume someone is watching. Use HTTPS sites only, avoid banking or admin work, or use your phone's data or a trusted VPN.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-network$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-network","title":"Networks, Wi-Fi and devices","minutes":20,"kind":"read","outline":"Firewalls, safe Wi-Fi, updates, backups and mobile device security for a small business.","body":"## How data travels\n\nYour phone talks to a website through many networks: your Wi-Fi router, your internet provider, undersea cables, the website's host. Anyone in the middle could read unencrypted traffic, which is why **HTTPS everywhere** matters.\n\nKey words:\n\n- **IP address**: the number that identifies a device on a network.\n- **Port**: a numbered \"door\" on a device for a service (443 for HTTPS, 22 for SSH).\n- **Firewall**: rules that decide which traffic may come in or go out. Close every port you do not need.\n- **VPN**: an encrypted tunnel, useful on public Wi-Fi and to reach office systems safely.\n\n## Wi-Fi for a shop or office\n\n1. Change the router's **default admin password** (attackers know them all).\n2. Use **WPA2 or WPA3** with a long passphrase. Never \"open\" or WEP.\n3. Put customers on a **guest network**, separate from the POS and office computers.\n4. Turn off **WPS** (the push-button pairing), which is easy to attack.\n5. Update the router's firmware.\n\n## Updates are security fixes\n\nMost attacks use holes that were **already fixed**. Turn on automatic updates for phones, computers, browsers and apps. Replace devices that no longer get updates.\n\n## Backups: the 3-2-1 rule\n\n- **3** copies of important data\n- on **2** different kinds of storage\n- with **1** copy **off-site** or offline (so ransomware cannot reach it)\n\nA backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. Test a restore every few months.\n\n## Phones and laptops\n\n- Screen lock with a PIN, fingerprint or face\n- Disk encryption on (most phones do this automatically with a screen lock)\n- Install apps only from the official store\n- \"Find my device\" on, so a lost phone can be locked or wiped\n- Separate staff accounts; no shared admin passwords\n\n## Public Wi-Fi\n\nAssume someone is watching. Use HTTPS sites only, avoid banking or admin work, or use your phone's data or a trusted VPN.","quiz":[{"q":"Which Wi-Fi setup is best for a shop?","options":["One open network for everyone","WPA3 or WPA2, with customers on a separate guest network","WEP with the default password"],"answer":1,"why":"Strong encryption plus separating customers from business devices."},{"q":"What is the \"1\" in the 3-2-1 backup rule?","options":["One password","One copy off-site or offline","One backup a year"],"answer":1,"why":"An off-site or offline copy survives fire, theft and ransomware."},{"q":"Why do updates matter for security?","options":["They add colours","Most attacks use holes that updates already fixed","They make the device slower"],"answer":1,"why":"Unpatched software is one of the commonest ways in."},{"q":"What does a firewall do?","options":["Cools the server","Decides which network traffic is allowed","Stores passwords"],"answer":1,"why":"Firewall rules allow or block traffic by address and port."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-incident$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Incident response: when something goes wrong","kind":"prompt","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Breaches happen. Speed decides the damage.\n\n**Incident response** is the plan you follow when you suspect an attack: a staff member clicked a phishing link, money left the account, files are encrypted, customers report strange messages from your number.\n\n## The 6 steps (NIST style)\n\n1. **Prepare**: know who to call, where backups are, how to reach your bank, and keep a list of every system and admin account.\n2. **Detect and analyse**: what happened, when, which accounts and devices? Keep notes with times.\n3. **Contain**: stop it spreading. Disconnect the infected computer from the network (do not switch it off: evidence lives in memory), reset passwords, sign out all sessions, freeze cards.\n4. **Eradicate**: remove the cause: malware, the attacker's access, the forwarding rule they added to the email.\n5. **Recover**: restore from clean backups, bring systems back carefully, watch for the attacker returning.\n6. **Lessons learned**: what failed, what will change? Update the plan.\n\n## Who to tell\n\n- Your **bank** immediately for any money movement (minutes matter for recalls)\n- **Customers** whose data was exposed, honestly and quickly, with what they should do\n- The **regulator** where the law requires it (in Nigeria, the NDPA 2023 requires notifying the Nigeria Data Protection Commission of serious personal data breaches within 72 hours)\n- The **police / cybercrime unit** for fraud\n\n## Evidence\n\nKeep screenshots, emails (with full headers), logs, transaction references and a timeline. Do not \"clean up\" before you have recorded what you saw.\n\n## Practise\n\nUse the tutor to build a one-page incident response plan for a real or imagined business. A good plan names people, phone numbers to call, and the first 5 actions in order.","practice":"Write a prompt asking the AI to create a one-page incident response plan for a business you know (say what the business is, how many staff, what systems it uses, and what format you want). Then ask it what the first 5 actions should be if a staff member reports clicking a phishing link.","track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":7}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-incident$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-incident","title":"Incident response: when something goes wrong","minutes":20,"kind":"prompt","outline":"The 6 steps of incident response, and practise writing a response plan with the AI tutor.","body":"## Breaches happen. Speed decides the damage.\n\n**Incident response** is the plan you follow when you suspect an attack: a staff member clicked a phishing link, money left the account, files are encrypted, customers report strange messages from your number.\n\n## The 6 steps (NIST style)\n\n1. **Prepare**: know who to call, where backups are, how to reach your bank, and keep a list of every system and admin account.\n2. **Detect and analyse**: what happened, when, which accounts and devices? Keep notes with times.\n3. **Contain**: stop it spreading. Disconnect the infected computer from the network (do not switch it off: evidence lives in memory), reset passwords, sign out all sessions, freeze cards.\n4. **Eradicate**: remove the cause: malware, the attacker's access, the forwarding rule they added to the email.\n5. **Recover**: restore from clean backups, bring systems back carefully, watch for the attacker returning.\n6. **Lessons learned**: what failed, what will change? Update the plan.\n\n## Who to tell\n\n- Your **bank** immediately for any money movement (minutes matter for recalls)\n- **Customers** whose data was exposed, honestly and quickly, with what they should do\n- The **regulator** where the law requires it (in Nigeria, the NDPA 2023 requires notifying the Nigeria Data Protection Commission of serious personal data breaches within 72 hours)\n- The **police / cybercrime unit** for fraud\n\n## Evidence\n\nKeep screenshots, emails (with full headers), logs, transaction references and a timeline. Do not \"clean up\" before you have recorded what you saw.\n\n## Practise\n\nUse the tutor to build a one-page incident response plan for a real or imagined business. A good plan names people, phone numbers to call, and the first 5 actions in order.","practice":"Write a prompt asking the AI to create a one-page incident response plan for a business you know (say what the business is, how many staff, what systems it uses, and what format you want). Then ask it what the first 5 actions should be if a staff member reports clicking a phishing link."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cyber-career$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Security careers, certifications and your portfolio","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Where security people work\n\n- **SOC analyst**: watches alerts in a Security Operations Centre and investigates the suspicious ones. A common first job.\n- **IT support with security duties**: sets up devices, accounts, backups and 2FA for a company.\n- **Penetration tester (ethical hacker)**: is paid to attack systems, with written permission, and report the holes.\n- **Security engineer**: builds the defences: firewalls, identity, cloud security.\n- **GRC (governance, risk, compliance)**: policies, audits, data protection (NDPA, GDPR, ISO 27001).\n- **Secure developer**: writes code that resists attacks (everything you practised with XSS and access control).\n\n## Certifications employers recognise\n\nStart: **CompTIA Security+**, **ISC2 Certified in Cybersecurity (CC)** (often free to sit), **Google Cybersecurity Certificate**. Later: CySA+, CEH, OSCP (hands-on hacking), cloud security certificates (AWS, Azure). Your CDM Studio certificate shows the foundations and is a good first line on your CV; follow it with one of these.\n\n## Build proof, not just certificates\n\n- Home lab: a spare laptop with free tools, practising on deliberately vulnerable apps (OWASP Juice Shop, DVWA)\n- Capture the Flag (CTF) games: TryHackMe, Hack The Box, picoCTF\n- Write up what you learned in short posts; share your CDM Studio projects (your link checker, strength meter, safe comment wall)\n- Help a local business: set up 2FA, backups and guest Wi-Fi, and write a one-page report\n\n## The law and ethics\n\n**Only test systems you own or have written permission to test.** In Nigeria the Cybercrimes Act makes unauthorised access a crime, even \"just to look\". Good security people are trusted because they follow this rule without exception.\n\n## Next steps\n\nFinish the final exam to earn your certificate, add it to LinkedIn and your CV, and pick your next certification.","practice":null,"track":"cyber","track_title":"Cybersecurity essentials","paid":true,"order":8}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cyber-career$cdm$, $cdm$cyber$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cyber-career","title":"Security careers, certifications and your portfolio","minutes":15,"kind":"read","outline":"Roles in security, the certifications employers ask for, and how to build proof of skill.","body":"## Where security people work\n\n- **SOC analyst**: watches alerts in a Security Operations Centre and investigates the suspicious ones. A common first job.\n- **IT support with security duties**: sets up devices, accounts, backups and 2FA for a company.\n- **Penetration tester (ethical hacker)**: is paid to attack systems, with written permission, and report the holes.\n- **Security engineer**: builds the defences: firewalls, identity, cloud security.\n- **GRC (governance, risk, compliance)**: policies, audits, data protection (NDPA, GDPR, ISO 27001).\n- **Secure developer**: writes code that resists attacks (everything you practised with XSS and access control).\n\n## Certifications employers recognise\n\nStart: **CompTIA Security+**, **ISC2 Certified in Cybersecurity (CC)** (often free to sit), **Google Cybersecurity Certificate**. Later: CySA+, CEH, OSCP (hands-on hacking), cloud security certificates (AWS, Azure). Your CDM Studio certificate shows the foundations and is a good first line on your CV; follow it with one of these.\n\n## Build proof, not just certificates\n\n- Home lab: a spare laptop with free tools, practising on deliberately vulnerable apps (OWASP Juice Shop, DVWA)\n- Capture the Flag (CTF) games: TryHackMe, Hack The Box, picoCTF\n- Write up what you learned in short posts; share your CDM Studio projects (your link checker, strength meter, safe comment wall)\n- Help a local business: set up 2FA, backups and guest Wi-Fi, and write a one-page report\n\n## The law and ethics\n\n**Only test systems you own or have written permission to test.** In Nigeria the Cybercrimes Act makes unauthorised access a crime, even \"just to look\". Good security people are trusted because they follow this rule without exception.\n\n## Next steps\n\nFinish the final exam to earn your certificate, add it to LinkedIn and your CV, and pick your next certification.","quiz":[{"q":"You find a website with a hole. What may you do?","options":["Test it fully to prove the point","Only test systems you own or have written permission to test","Post the hole on social media"],"answer":1,"why":"Unauthorised access is illegal even with good intentions; report responsibly instead."},{"q":"A good first security job that watches and investigates alerts is…","options":["SOC analyst","Graphic designer","Database clerk"],"answer":0,"why":"Security Operations Centre analysts triage alerts; many careers start here."},{"q":"Which is a widely recognised entry-level security certification?","options":["CompTIA Security+","ICAN ATS","IELTS"],"answer":0,"why":"Security+ (and ISC2 CC) are common entry-level certificates."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
