insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-basics$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"What \"the cloud\" really is","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Someone else's computers, rented by the minute\n\n\"The cloud\" is large data centres run by companies such as **Amazon Web Services (AWS)**, **Microsoft Azure** and **Google Cloud**, plus many smaller providers. Instead of buying servers, you rent computing, storage and services and pay for what you use.\n\nWhy businesses use it:\n\n- **No big upfront cost**: start small, grow when customers grow\n- **Scale**: handle a Black Friday rush, then scale back down\n- **Reliability**: data copied across buildings and cities\n- **Speed**: a new server or database in minutes, not weeks\n\n## Regions and availability zones\n\nProviders group data centres into **regions** (for example Europe-West, South Africa, US-East), each with several **availability zones** (separate buildings with their own power). Pick a region close to your users for speed, and consider where data must live by law (Nigeria's NDPA has rules on transferring personal data abroad).\n\n## Service models\n\n- **IaaS** (infrastructure): you rent virtual machines and manage the operating system yourself. *AWS EC2, Azure VMs.*\n- **PaaS** (platform): you give your code; the provider runs it. *Netlify, Vercel, Heroku, Supabase, Google App Engine.*\n- **SaaS** (software): you just use the app. *Gmail, Microsoft 365, CDM ERP.*\n- **Serverless / functions**: small pieces of code that run on demand and cost nothing when idle. *Supabase Edge Functions, AWS Lambda.* CDM Studio's AI builder runs this way.\n\n## Shared responsibility\n\nThe provider secures the buildings, hardware and its platform. **You** remain responsible for your accounts, passwords, access rights, data, and how you configure services. Most cloud breaches come from **customer mistakes**: a public storage bucket, a leaked key, an admin account without two-step sign-in.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-git$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Git and version control","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Never lose work again\n\n**Git** records every change to your project as a **commit** (a snapshot with a message). You can see who changed what and why, and go back to any earlier version. **GitHub**, **GitLab** and **Bitbucket** host Git repositories online and add collaboration tools.\n\n## The core commands\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/you/shop.git   # copy a repository to your computer\ngit status                                   # what changed?\ngit add index.html                           # choose changes for the next commit\ngit commit -m \"Add WhatsApp order button\"   # save a snapshot with a clear message\ngit push                                     # send commits to GitHub\ngit pull                                     # get others' commits\n```\n\n## Branches and pull requests\n\nA **branch** is a separate line of work. The team keeps `main` working at all times:\n\n1. Create a branch: `git switch -c add-delivery-fee`\n2. Commit your changes there\n3. Push and open a **pull request (PR)**: teammates review, automated tests run\n4. **Merge** into main when approved\n\nIf two people change the same lines, Git reports a **merge conflict**; you choose the correct version and commit.\n\n## Good habits\n\n- Small commits with messages that say **why**: *\"Fix VAT rounding on receipts\"*, not *\"update\"*\n- Never commit secrets (API keys, passwords, `.env` files). Use a `.gitignore` and the platform's secret store. If a key is ever pushed, **rotate it** (make a new one): deleting the commit is not enough, history keeps it.\n- Protect `main`: require reviews and passing tests before merging\n\n## Where you have seen this\n\nCDM's own products are built this way: each change is a branch and a pull request, tests run on GitHub, and merging deploys the site.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-cicd$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"CI/CD: automatic tests and deployment","kind":"code","task":"Write run(steps) and order(jobs).","hint":"order: const out = []; while (out.length < names.length) { const next = names.find(n => !out.includes(n) && jobs[n].every(d => out.includes(d))); if (!next) throw new Error('cycle'); out.push(next) }","body":"## Continuous integration and continuous delivery\n\n- **CI**: every push runs automated checks (install, lint, tests, build). Problems are found in minutes, not by customers.\n- **CD**: when checks pass on main, the app is deployed automatically (to a test environment, then production).\n\nA pipeline is an ordered list of **steps**, often grouped in **jobs** that can depend on each other. If a step fails, the pipeline stops and nothing broken is deployed. CDM Studio itself deploys like this: tests, then the database, then server functions, then the website.\n\nA GitHub Actions file looks like:\n\n```yaml\non: push\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm test\n  deploy:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: ./deploy.sh\n```\n\n## Your task: a tiny pipeline runner\n\n`run(steps)` gets an array of `{ name, run }` where `run()` returns true (pass) or false (fail), or throws.\n\n- Run steps **in order**; stop at the first failure (false or an error).\n- Return `{ ok, ran, failed }`: `ran` lists the names of steps that were run, `failed` is the failing step's name (or null).\n\nThen `order(jobs)`: `jobs` is an object like `{ test: [], deploy: ['test'], build: ['test'] }` mapping each job to the jobs it needs. Return a list of job names where every job comes after all its needs (keep the object's order when there is a choice). If there is a cycle, throw an Error.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cloud-cicd$cdm$, $cdm$cloud$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cloud-cicd","title":"CI/CD: automatic tests and deployment","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"How pipelines work, and write a pipeline runner that stops at the first failing step.","body":"## Continuous integration and continuous delivery\n\n- **CI**: every push runs automated checks (install, lint, tests, build). Problems are found in minutes, not by customers.\n- **CD**: when checks pass on main, the app is deployed automatically (to a test environment, then production).\n\nA pipeline is an ordered list of **steps**, often grouped in **jobs** that can depend on each other. If a step fails, the pipeline stops and nothing broken is deployed. CDM Studio itself deploys like this: tests, then the database, then server functions, then the website.\n\nA GitHub Actions file looks like:\n\n```yaml\non: push\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm test\n  deploy:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: ./deploy.sh\n```\n\n## Your task: a tiny pipeline runner\n\n`run(steps)` gets an array of `{ name, run }` where `run()` returns true (pass) or false (fail), or throws.\n\n- Run steps **in order**; stop at the first failure (false or an error).\n- Return `{ ok, ran, failed }`: `ran` lists the names of steps that were run, `failed` is the failing step's name (or null).\n\nThen `order(jobs)`: `jobs` is an object like `{ test: [], deploy: ['test'], build: ['test'] }` mapping each job to the jobs it needs. Return a list of job names where every job comes after all its needs (keep the object's order when there is a choice). If there is a cycle, throw an Error.","task":"Write run(steps) and order(jobs).","starter":"<script>\n  function run(steps) {\n  }\n  function order(jobs) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"all steps pass","test":"const r = run([{ name: 'install', run: () => true }, { name: 'test', run: () => true }]); return r.ok && r.ran.join() === 'install,test' && r.failed === null"},{"label":"stops at the first failure","test":"const r = run([{ name: 'install', run: () => true }, { name: 'test', run: () => false }, { name: 'deploy', run: () => true }]); return !r.ok && r.ran.join() === 'install,test' && r.failed === 'test'"},{"label":"a thrown error is a failure","test":"const r = run([{ name: 'build', run: () => { throw new Error('x') } }, { name: 'deploy', run: () => true }]); return !r.ok && r.failed === 'build' && r.ran.length === 1"},{"label":"jobs come after their needs","test":"const o = order({ deploy: ['build', 'test'], build: ['test'], test: [] }); return o.join() === 'test,build,deploy'"},{"label":"cycles are refused","test":"try { order({ a: ['b'], b: ['a'] }); return false } catch { return true }"}],"hint":"order: const out = []; while (out.length < names.length) { const next = names.find(n => !out.includes(n) && jobs[n].every(d => out.includes(d))); if (!next) throw new Error('cycle'); out.push(next) }"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-containers$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Containers, Docker and Kubernetes","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## \"It works on my computer\"\n\nApps depend on a specific operating system, language version and libraries. A **container** packages the app **with everything it needs**, so it runs the same on a laptop, a test server and the cloud.\n\n- An **image** is the packaged recipe (read-only).\n- A **container** is a running copy of an image. You can run many containers from one image.\n- **Docker** is the most common tool to build and run them. Images are shared through **registries** (Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry).\n\n## A Dockerfile, line by line\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine          # start from a small image with Node.js\nWORKDIR /app                 # work inside /app\nCOPY package*.json ./        # copy the dependency list first (better caching)\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev        # install production dependencies\nCOPY . .                     # copy the app code\nUSER node                    # do not run as root (safer)\nEXPOSE 3000                  # the app listens on port 3000\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]    # what to run when the container starts\n```\n\n`docker build -t shop-api .` makes the image; `docker run -p 3000:3000 shop-api` starts a container.\n\n## Containers vs virtual machines\n\nA VM includes a whole operating system (heavy, minutes to start). Containers share the host's kernel (light, seconds to start), so you can run many more on the same machine.\n\n## Kubernetes in one paragraph\n\nWhen you run dozens or thousands of containers, **Kubernetes** (K8s) schedules them across many machines, restarts crashed ones, scales them with traffic, and rolls out new versions gradually. Cloud providers offer it managed (EKS, AKS, GKE). Many small teams never need it: a PaaS or serverless platform is simpler.\n\n## Security basics\n\nUse small official base images, update them, never bake secrets into images (pass them at run time), and do not run as root.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cloud-containers$cdm$, $cdm$cloud$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cloud-containers","title":"Containers, Docker and Kubernetes","minutes":20,"kind":"read","outline":"Why containers exist, images vs containers, a Dockerfile explained, and what Kubernetes adds.","body":"## \"It works on my computer\"\n\nApps depend on a specific operating system, language version and libraries. A **container** packages the app **with everything it needs**, so it runs the same on a laptop, a test server and the cloud.\n\n- An **image** is the packaged recipe (read-only).\n- A **container** is a running copy of an image. You can run many containers from one image.\n- **Docker** is the most common tool to build and run them. Images are shared through **registries** (Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry).\n\n## A Dockerfile, line by line\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine          # start from a small image with Node.js\nWORKDIR /app                 # work inside /app\nCOPY package*.json ./        # copy the dependency list first (better caching)\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev        # install production dependencies\nCOPY . .                     # copy the app code\nUSER node                    # do not run as root (safer)\nEXPOSE 3000                  # the app listens on port 3000\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]    # what to run when the container starts\n```\n\n`docker build -t shop-api .` makes the image; `docker run -p 3000:3000 shop-api` starts a container.\n\n## Containers vs virtual machines\n\nA VM includes a whole operating system (heavy, minutes to start). Containers share the host's kernel (light, seconds to start), so you can run many more on the same machine.\n\n## Kubernetes in one paragraph\n\nWhen you run dozens or thousands of containers, **Kubernetes** (K8s) schedules them across many machines, restarts crashed ones, scales them with traffic, and rolls out new versions gradually. Cloud providers offer it managed (EKS, AKS, GKE). Many small teams never need it: a PaaS or serverless platform is simpler.\n\n## Security basics\n\nUse small official base images, update them, never bake secrets into images (pass them at run time), and do not run as root.","quiz":[{"q":"An image vs a container:","options":["They are the same","An image is the recipe; a container is a running copy","A container builds images"],"answer":1,"why":"Many containers can run from one image."},{"q":"Why \"USER node\" in a Dockerfile?","options":["Faster builds","Not running as root limits damage if the app is hacked","It is required"],"answer":1,"why":"Least privilege inside the container."},{"q":"Where should an API key for a container go?","options":["Inside the image","Passed at run time from a secret store","In the Dockerfile comments"],"answer":1,"why":"Images are shared; secrets must not be baked in."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-config$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Configuration, environments and secrets","kind":"code","task":"Write checkConfig(env, spec).","hint":"For each item: const v = env[s.name]; if (v === undefined || v === '') { if (s.required) errors.push(s.name + ' is missing'); summary[s.name] = null; continue } ... summary[s.name] = s.secret ? '***' : v","body":"## Same code, different settings\n\nAn app runs in several **environments**: your laptop (development), **staging** (a copy for testing), and **production** (real customers). The code is the same; the **configuration** differs: database address, payment keys (test vs live), email sender.\n\nThe widely used **twelve-factor** rule: keep configuration in **environment variables**, not in the code. Secrets (keys, passwords, tokens) belong in the platform's **secret store** (GitHub Actions secrets, Supabase secrets, AWS Secrets Manager), never in Git, never in a web page.\n\n## Fail fast, never leak\n\nAt start-up, an app should check its configuration and refuse to start if something important is missing or wrong, with a clear message. That message must **never print secret values**: logs are read by many people and tools.\n\n## Your task\n\n`checkConfig(env, spec)`:\n\n- `spec` describes each variable: `{ name, required, type: 'string' | 'number' | 'url', secret }`.\n- Return `{ ok, errors, summary }`:\n  - `errors`: messages like `\"PORT must be a number\"`, `\"DB_URL is missing\"`, `\"SITE_URL must be an https url\"`.\n  - `summary`: an object of the values to log, where **secrets are shown as** `\"***\"` and missing values as `null`.\n- Numbers: the text must be a valid finite number. URLs: must start with `https://` and parse with `new URL`.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cloud-config$cdm$, $cdm$cloud$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cloud-config","title":"Configuration, environments and secrets","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Twelve-factor config, dev/staging/production, and validate an app's environment variables safely.","body":"## Same code, different settings\n\nAn app runs in several **environments**: your laptop (development), **staging** (a copy for testing), and **production** (real customers). The code is the same; the **configuration** differs: database address, payment keys (test vs live), email sender.\n\nThe widely used **twelve-factor** rule: keep configuration in **environment variables**, not in the code. Secrets (keys, passwords, tokens) belong in the platform's **secret store** (GitHub Actions secrets, Supabase secrets, AWS Secrets Manager), never in Git, never in a web page.\n\n## Fail fast, never leak\n\nAt start-up, an app should check its configuration and refuse to start if something important is missing or wrong, with a clear message. That message must **never print secret values**: logs are read by many people and tools.\n\n## Your task\n\n`checkConfig(env, spec)`:\n\n- `spec` describes each variable: `{ name, required, type: 'string' | 'number' | 'url', secret }`.\n- Return `{ ok, errors, summary }`:\n  - `errors`: messages like `\"PORT must be a number\"`, `\"DB_URL is missing\"`, `\"SITE_URL must be an https url\"`.\n  - `summary`: an object of the values to log, where **secrets are shown as** `\"***\"` and missing values as `null`.\n- Numbers: the text must be a valid finite number. URLs: must start with `https://` and parse with `new URL`.","task":"Write checkConfig(env, spec).","starter":"<script>\n  const SPEC = [\n    { name: 'PORT', required: true, type: 'number' },\n    { name: 'SITE_URL', required: true, type: 'url' },\n    { name: 'PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY', required: true, type: 'string', secret: true },\n    { name: 'SENTRY_DSN', required: false, type: 'url' },\n  ]\n  function checkConfig(env, spec) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"a good config passes","test":"const r = checkConfig({ PORT: '3000', SITE_URL: 'https://shop.ng', PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY: 'sk_live_x' }, SPEC); return r.ok && r.errors.length === 0"},{"label":"missing required values are errors","test":"const r = checkConfig({ PORT: '3000', SITE_URL: 'https://shop.ng' }, SPEC); return !r.ok && r.errors.includes('PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY is missing')"},{"label":"types are checked","test":"const r = checkConfig({ PORT: 'abc', SITE_URL: 'http://shop.ng', PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY: 'x' }, SPEC); return r.errors.includes('PORT must be a number') && r.errors.includes('SITE_URL must be an https url')"},{"label":"secrets never appear in the summary","test":"const r = checkConfig({ PORT: '1', SITE_URL: 'https://a.ng', PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY: 'sk_live_TOPSECRET' }, SPEC); return r.summary.PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY === '***' && !JSON.stringify(r).includes('TOPSECRET')"},{"label":"optional missing values are fine and null","test":"const r = checkConfig({ PORT: '1', SITE_URL: 'https://a.ng', PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY: 'k' }, SPEC); return r.ok && r.summary.SENTRY_DSN === null"}],"hint":"For each item: const v = env[s.name]; if (v === undefined || v === '') { if (s.required) errors.push(s.name + ' is missing'); summary[s.name] = null; continue } ... summary[s.name] = s.secret ? '***' : v"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-monitoring$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Monitoring, logs and uptime","kind":"code","task":"Write uptime, downtimeBudget and errorSpikes.","hint":"errorSpikes: const n = {}; for (const l of lines) if (l.includes(' ERROR ')) { const m = l.slice(0, 16); n[m] = (n[m] || 0) + 1 } return Object.keys(n).filter(m => n[m] > limit).sort()","body":"## You cannot fix what you cannot see\n\nProduction systems need:\n\n- **Logs**: what happened (requests, errors) with timestamps. Never log passwords, card numbers or secrets.\n- **Metrics**: numbers over time (requests per minute, response time, error rate, CPU).\n- **Traces**: one request's journey through several services.\n- **Alerts**: a message (email, Slack, phone) when something crosses a threshold. Alert on what users feel (errors, slowness), not on every small blip, or people start ignoring alerts.\n- **Uptime checks**: a service pings your site every minute from several countries.\n\nTools: Sentry (errors), Grafana and Prometheus (metrics), Better Stack or UptimeRobot (uptime), and each cloud's own monitoring.\n\n## SLAs and error budgets\n\nAn **SLA** promises availability, for example **99.9%** a month. That allows about **43 minutes** of downtime in a 30-day month. The allowed downtime is the **error budget**: if you have used most of it, slow down risky releases.\n\n| Availability | Downtime per 30 days |\n|---|---|\n| 99% | 7.2 hours |\n| 99.9% | 43.2 minutes |\n| 99.99% | 4.3 minutes |\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `uptime(checks)`: `checks` is an array of booleans (true = the site answered). Return the availability percentage rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. `downtimeBudget(slaPercent, days)`: allowed downtime in minutes, rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `errorSpikes(lines, limit)`: `lines` are log strings like `\"2026-11-20T10:01:12Z ERROR payment failed\"`. Count `ERROR` lines per minute (the first 16 characters, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM`) and return the minutes with **more than** `limit` errors, in time order.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cloud-monitoring$cdm$, $cdm$cloud$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cloud-monitoring","title":"Monitoring, logs and uptime","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"SLAs, error budgets and alerting; compute uptime and find error spikes in logs.","body":"## You cannot fix what you cannot see\n\nProduction systems need:\n\n- **Logs**: what happened (requests, errors) with timestamps. Never log passwords, card numbers or secrets.\n- **Metrics**: numbers over time (requests per minute, response time, error rate, CPU).\n- **Traces**: one request's journey through several services.\n- **Alerts**: a message (email, Slack, phone) when something crosses a threshold. Alert on what users feel (errors, slowness), not on every small blip, or people start ignoring alerts.\n- **Uptime checks**: a service pings your site every minute from several countries.\n\nTools: Sentry (errors), Grafana and Prometheus (metrics), Better Stack or UptimeRobot (uptime), and each cloud's own monitoring.\n\n## SLAs and error budgets\n\nAn **SLA** promises availability, for example **99.9%** a month. That allows about **43 minutes** of downtime in a 30-day month. The allowed downtime is the **error budget**: if you have used most of it, slow down risky releases.\n\n| Availability | Downtime per 30 days |\n|---|---|\n| 99% | 7.2 hours |\n| 99.9% | 43.2 minutes |\n| 99.99% | 4.3 minutes |\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `uptime(checks)`: `checks` is an array of booleans (true = the site answered). Return the availability percentage rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. `downtimeBudget(slaPercent, days)`: allowed downtime in minutes, rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `errorSpikes(lines, limit)`: `lines` are log strings like `\"2026-11-20T10:01:12Z ERROR payment failed\"`. Count `ERROR` lines per minute (the first 16 characters, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM`) and return the minutes with **more than** `limit` errors, in time order.","task":"Write uptime, downtimeBudget and errorSpikes.","starter":"<script>\n  function uptime(checks) {\n  }\n  function downtimeBudget(slaPercent, days) {\n  }\n  function errorSpikes(lines, limit) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"uptime percentage","test":"return uptime([true, true, true, false]) === 75 && uptime(Array(1000).fill(true).fill(false, 0, 1)) === 99.9"},{"label":"99.9% over 30 days is 43.2 minutes","test":"return downtimeBudget(99.9, 30) === 43.2 && downtimeBudget(99, 30) === 432"},{"label":"error spikes per minute","test":"const L = ['2026-11-20T10:01:01Z ERROR a', '2026-11-20T10:01:30Z ERROR b', '2026-11-20T10:01:59Z INFO ok', '2026-11-20T10:02:00Z ERROR c', '2026-11-20T10:00:10Z ERROR d', '2026-11-20T10:00:11Z ERROR e']; return errorSpikes(L, 1).join() === '2026-11-20T10:00,2026-11-20T10:01'"},{"label":"only ERROR lines count","test":"return errorSpikes(['2026-11-20T10:01:01Z WARN x', '2026-11-20T10:01:02Z INFO y'], 0).length === 0"}],"hint":"errorSpikes: const n = {}; for (const l of lines) if (l.includes(' ERROR ')) { const m = l.slice(0, 16); n[m] = (n[m] || 0) + 1 } return Object.keys(n).filter(m => n[m] > limit).sort()"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$cloud-cost$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Cloud costs, backups and your cloud career","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## The cloud bill surprise\n\nPay-as-you-go cuts both ways. Common surprises: servers left running over the weekend, oversized databases, data transfer out of the cloud, forgotten test environments, and logs kept forever.\n\nControl costs with:\n\n- **Budgets and alerts**: an email when spending passes 50%, 80% and 100% of the monthly budget\n- **Right-sizing**: use the smallest size that meets the need; scale automatically\n- **Free tiers and serverless** for small apps (many CDM products run on free or cheap plans)\n- **Tagging** every resource with its project and owner, so you know what to delete\n- **Reserved / committed use** discounts for steady workloads\n- Exchange rates: bills are in dollars; plan for the naira moving\n\n## Backups and disaster recovery\n\nTwo numbers define your plan:\n\n- **RPO** (recovery point objective): how much data can you afford to lose? 24 hours means daily backups are enough; 5 minutes needs continuous replication.\n- **RTO** (recovery time objective): how long can you be down? This decides how ready your standby must be.\n\nFollow 3-2-1 (three copies, two media, one off-site), keep backups in a different account or region, and **test restores**.\n\n## Cloud careers\n\n- Entry: **cloud support associate**, **IT administrator**, **junior DevOps**\n- Certifications: **AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner** → **AWS Solutions Architect Associate**; **Microsoft AZ-900** → **AZ-104**; **Google Cloud Digital Leader** → **Associate Cloud Engineer**; for containers, **CKA** (Kubernetes)\n- Portfolio: deploy a real app with CI/CD, a custom domain, monitoring and a budget alert, and write up the architecture with a diagram. Your CDM Studio projects can be hosted on your own domain from the project's More menu.","practice":null,"track":"cloud","track_title":"Cloud and DevOps essentials","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$cloud-cost$cdm$, $cdm$cloud$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"cloud-cost","title":"Cloud costs, backups and your cloud career","minutes":20,"kind":"read","outline":"Keep cloud bills under control, plan backups and recovery, and the certifications that open cloud jobs.","body":"## The cloud bill surprise\n\nPay-as-you-go cuts both ways. Common surprises: servers left running over the weekend, oversized databases, data transfer out of the cloud, forgotten test environments, and logs kept forever.\n\nControl costs with:\n\n- **Budgets and alerts**: an email when spending passes 50%, 80% and 100% of the monthly budget\n- **Right-sizing**: use the smallest size that meets the need; scale automatically\n- **Free tiers and serverless** for small apps (many CDM products run on free or cheap plans)\n- **Tagging** every resource with its project and owner, so you know what to delete\n- **Reserved / committed use** discounts for steady workloads\n- Exchange rates: bills are in dollars; plan for the naira moving\n\n## Backups and disaster recovery\n\nTwo numbers define your plan:\n\n- **RPO** (recovery point objective): how much data can you afford to lose? 24 hours means daily backups are enough; 5 minutes needs continuous replication.\n- **RTO** (recovery time objective): how long can you be down? This decides how ready your standby must be.\n\nFollow 3-2-1 (three copies, two media, one off-site), keep backups in a different account or region, and **test restores**.\n\n## Cloud careers\n\n- Entry: **cloud support associate**, **IT administrator**, **junior DevOps**\n- Certifications: **AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner** → **AWS Solutions Architect Associate**; **Microsoft AZ-900** → **AZ-104**; **Google Cloud Digital Leader** → **Associate Cloud Engineer**; for containers, **CKA** (Kubernetes)\n- Portfolio: deploy a real app with CI/CD, a custom domain, monitoring and a budget alert, and write up the architecture with a diagram. Your CDM Studio projects can be hosted on your own domain from the project's More menu.","quiz":[{"q":"The cheapest way to stop surprise bills is…","options":["Budgets with alerts and deleting unused resources","Bigger servers","Turning off monitoring"],"answer":0,"why":"Visibility plus cleanup."},{"q":"RPO of 1 hour means…","options":["You can be down for 1 hour","You can lose at most 1 hour of data","Backups once a day are fine"],"answer":1,"why":"RPO is about data loss; RTO is about downtime."},{"q":"A good first AWS certification is…","options":["AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner","CKA","PL-300"],"answer":0,"why":"It is the foundational AWS exam."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
